  "id": "pinkillu_norte_potosi",
  "name": "Pinkillu (Norte Potosí rainy-season flutes and wayñu)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Rainy-season music of the Quechua-speaking ayllus around Macha in Norte Potosí, Bolivia, documented by the ethnomusicologist Henry Stobart in Music and the Poetics of Production in the Bolivian Andes. Through the growing season leading up to Carnival, men play consorts of pinkillu cane duct flutes in several sizes and young women sing wayñu songs to their tunes, music bound up with the growth of the crops; in the dry season other instruments, among them the julajula panpipes, take their place. The bamboo-flute card stands in for the pinkillu.",
